    {
      "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alexar76/agent-skills-state-2026/74279108cb0b9ee72e9435d237e97444494f4e8f/docs/skills-with-unscoped-bash.md",
      "fetched_at_utc": "2026-10-26T20:03:00.531226+00:00",
      "status": 200,
      "sha256": "1dc8c3c30e4b4b1305c33d80fb5d4eb7f30880cabaa172ee3e5eed34d5a1688b",
      "opus_lines": [
        {
          "line": 30,
          "text": "Claude Code and similar hosts treat `allowed-tools` as pre-approval: while a skill is active, the tools it lists run without the usual permission prompt. With a scoped rule such as `Bash(npm test:*)` the host still asks before anything else. With bare `Bash` it does not ask at all, so the only review a command gets is whatever the user did before installing the skill. A skill's instructions can also be changed by the content it reads (a web page, an issue, a file in the repository), so an unscoped grant turns a prompt injection that reaches the skill into shell access."
        },
        {
          "line": 110,
          "text": "- **Scripts outside the skill folder are not read.** Many skills call tools in a plugin's shared folder or under the user's home directory (`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/tools/...`, `~/.claude/skills/...`). Those commands count as heads, but their contents were not fetched, so script-level behaviours are a lower bound."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "# What do skills that ask for the whole shell do with it? Unscoped Bash in 3,715 public skills, October 2026",
      "report_line_count": 126
    }
